<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Anuradhapura Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">437 BCE – 1017 CE (approx.) · capital Anuradhapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sri Lanka's longest-lived kingdom, where Theravada Buddhism took root and kings built vast irrigation tanks and giant stupas over 1,400 years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The chronicles say King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandukabhaya">Pandukabhaya</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> around 437 BCE, succeeding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tambapanni">Kingdom of Tambapanni</a>. In the 3rd century BCE, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanampiya-tissa">Devanampiya Tissa</a>, the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahinda-buddhist-monk">Mahinda</a>, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and a sapling of the Bodhi tree was planted in the city. Around 161 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutugemunu">Dutugemunu</a> defeated the Tamil ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellalan">Elara</a> and united the island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled with close ties to the Buddhist sangha; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> and Abhayagiri monasteries were rival centres of learning, and the Pali Canon was first written down in the 1st century BCE. Huge stupas such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruwanwelisaya">Ruwanwelisaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jetavanaramaya">Jetavanaramaya</a> rose in the capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> farming depended on great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kala-wewa">Kala Wewa</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhatusena">Dhatusena</a>, and ports traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Repeated invasions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a> weakened the kingdom. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in 993, and in 1017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra I</a> captured King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahinda-v">Mahinda V</a>. The Sinhalese capital later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/polonnaruwa-kingdom">Polonnaruwa Kingdom</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/anuradhapura-kingdom.jpg" alt="Anuradhapura Kingdom" width="320" height="489"><figcaption>Image: Mithila Madawa Gunathilake · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anuradhapura_Kingdom_(3rd_century_BC_map).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>437 BCE – 1017 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Anuradhapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Devanampiya Tissa, Dutugemunu, Dhatusena</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sinhala</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Anuradhapura, Rajarata (Anuradhapura period)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=290&amp;focus=anuradhapura-kingdom" aria-label="Anuradhapura Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6902 1866 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Anuradhapura Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y